    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, i'm looking for some advice on pallet stacking for our new high-density racking system in the warehouse. we're dealing with some pretty heavy industrial loads and i'm worried about stability when we stack them high. specifically, what's the best way to handle the center of gravity and any interlocking patterns you'd recommend to keep things from shifting?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For heavy industrial loads in high-density racking, you must treat the load like a single, very large, and potentially unstable mass. Keep the center of gravity as low as possible, ideally centered directly over the pallet footprint, and never stack heavier items on top of lighter ones. If you have a mix of loads, ensure the heaviest ones are at the base layer, and consider using interlocking patterns that distribute weight evenly across the racking beams.\n\nIt's crucial to check the load capacity ratings of your specific racking system for those high stacks, as overloading is a major risk. Furthermore, ensure your forklifts are operating within safe load limits and are trained on proper load handling techniques. A slight tilt or uneven load distribution can lead to catastrophic failure, so precision is key.",
