    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah we checked the surfaces and the racking seems okay/level. it's just weird. it feels almost like something is messing with the balance or causing it to tip just a tiny bit when we aren't looking. could there be some kind of vibration or external factor causing it to unbalance like that?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Vibration is a very real culprit in high-density storage, especially if your warehouse floor is near heavy machinery or if the forklifts are operating aggressively. Even minor, constant vibrations can cause a slow, cumulative shift in the center of gravity, leading to that subtle leaning you're observing. It's like a tiny, persistent nudge from a very small, unseen force.\n\nYou should investigate if the racking system has adequate dampening or if the floor slab itself is transmitting excessive energy. Sometimes, the way the pallets are wrapped or shrink-wrapped can also contribute to shifting if the external pressure is unevenly applied. If the issue persists, consider implementing a periodic, gentle \"settling\" procedure where the stacks are briefly checked and nudged back into perfect alignment during non-operational hours.",
